# Unity Game Development Guidelines

This project is a multiplayer minigame playlist platform (similar to GTA V's Playlist system) built in Unity 6 using URP, the New Input System, Starter Assets (First Person Controller), Cinemachine, and CharacterController-based movement (not Rigidbody locomotion).

The platform supports multiple game modes, each with its own scene. Host can cherry-pick game modes to create custom playlists. RunnerSabotager is one of many game modes in the collection.

## Authoritative Architecture Rule

The latest STATE_vX file in [Assets/ProjectFiles](mdc:Assets/ProjectFiles) is the single source of truth for the project architecture. Before making architectural or structural decisions, consult the latest STATE_vX snapshot. Systems defined there are assumed implemented and working unless explicitly stated otherwise. Do not redesign, replace, or refactor systems defined in STATE_vX unless the user explicitly requests refactoring. All guidance must align with the current defined architecture.

## Project Architecture

- Multiplayer minigame playlist platform
- Each game mode has its own scene
- MatchSessionManager handles playlist progression
- Host can customize playlist in lobby (planned feature)
- CharacterController-based player movement
- Custom knockback system using force vectors
- Layer-based interaction system (game-mode specific, e.g., Runner / Sabotager roles)
- GameState system with persistent Bootstrap scene
- GameStateController singleton (DontDestroyOnLoad)
- Centralized scene transitions
- SceneNames constants (no magic strings)
- Scene self-registration (MainMenu, Lobby, Gameplay)
- Flow: Boot → MainMenu → Lobby → LoadingGame → Gameplay (via playlist)
- Scene loading currently single-mode (no additive system)
- Multiplayer with Netcode for GameObjects
- Game modes are independent and can be mixed in playlists

## Operational Behavior

- Provide implementation-ready guidance strictly within this stack.
- Never overwhelm with unnecessary information.
- Never ask unnecessary clarification questions.
- Solve only the exact problem requested.
- If required information is missing, make the most technically reasonable assumption and proceed unless ambiguity would materially change the solution.
- Do not propose alternative approaches unless explicitly requested.
- Remain strictly within Unity gameplay engineering for this project context unless explicitly instructed otherwise.
